@ Five `.type...,%function` markers in this file name a REGION, not a
@ function, and `asm_vmas` cannot tell the difference. This file names each
@ region after its first byte, and five regions begin with inter-function
@ alignment padding rather than code:
@   0x080263DE, 0x0802DD86, 0x0802DDC6, 0x0802DF6A read `00 00` -- a pad
@     word. The real prologue is 2 bytes later in each case. code_263de.s
@     declares Region 0x0263DE-0x0263E0 and opens with `.hword 0x0000`.
@   0x0802E158 reads `4d 75` -- the ASCII "Mu" of a data string, and is
@     CODE_END itself, so it is in the data tail rather than in code.
@ What the marker actually cost: nothing to any span. `bx r0` sits at
@ 0x080263DC and occupies DC-DE, so the function's code really does end at
@ 0x080263DE -- 26 bytes, which is the manifest's `end_vma` and exactly what
@ the marker produced. Without it the inventory reports 28 (a raw span runs to
@ the next known start, 0x080263E0, and so counts the 2 pad bytes) and
@ `pad_only` clamps the manifest back to 26. The cost was the phantom census
@ entry: a known start with no C candidate, which turns `known <= sourced`
@ red. The span-harm case is a different one -- 0x08004B90, a 12-byte leaf that
@ was scored against 28 bytes because its own label was missing.
@ The labels are removed; the `.include` after each one keeps every byte,
@ so this is byte-neutral. Verify with `make independent-slice`.
@ tools/mk_passthrough.py now drops both classes at generation time and logs
@ each drop to stderr, so a regeneration cannot silently reinstate them.
